<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Appenzell Ausserrhoden</h1><p class="st-meta">1597 CE – 1798 CE · capital Trogen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Reformed half of Appenzell, created in 1597, which became one of the most industrialised rural areas of early modern Europe.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-appenzell">Canton of Appenzell</a> was divided in 1597, the outer districts, which had adopted the Reformed faith, formed Ausserrhoden. Without the old capital, its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landsgemeinde">Landsgemeinde</a> met alternately in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trogen">Trogen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundwil">Hundwil</a>, and government business was shared between Trogen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herisau">Herisau</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>From the 17th century the villagers spun and wove linen and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> at home, and merchant families such as the Zellwegers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trogen">Trogen</a> grew rich in international trade. Government lay with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landsgemeinde">Landsgemeinde</a> and councils, and the half-canton shared a vote with Innerrhoden at the federal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagsatzung">Diet</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1798 it was absorbed into the canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canton-of-santis">Säntis</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a>, and in 1803 it was restored as a half-canton. Ausserrhoden abolished its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landsgemeinde">Landsgemeinde</a> in 1997 and remains a Swiss canton.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/appenzell-ausserrhoden.jpg" alt="Appenzell Ausserrhoden" width="320" height="389"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wappen_Appenzell_Ausserrhoden_matt.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1597 CE – 1798 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Trogen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Reformed Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swiss German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Outer Rhodes</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1698&amp;focus=appenzell-ausserrhoden" aria-label="Appenzell Ausserrhoden on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4901 515 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Appenzell Ausserrhoden lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5221 771L5220 773M5221 771L5186 782M5221 771L5188 772"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5220 773h0M5186 782h0M5188 772h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5221 771h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5221 771h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Trogen. <a href="/atlas?year=1698&amp;focus=appenzell-ausserrhoden">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
